<div class="interactive-textbook">

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Introduction</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      Electrostatics is the study of electric charges at rest. It deals with the forces between charges, the electric fields they create, and the behavior of conductors and insulators in the presence of electric fields.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Definition</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      Coulomb's Law describes the electrostatic force of interaction between charged particles.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Key Concepts</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      <ul>
        <li><b>Electric Charge:</b> A fundamental property of matter that can be positive or negative.</li>
        <li><b>Electrostatic Force:</b> The force of attraction or repulsion between charged objects.</li>
        <li><b>Distance:</b> The separation between the charged objects.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Formula</div>
    <div class="formula-card">
      <div class="formula-title">Coulomb's Law</div>
      <div class="formula-content">
        $$
        F = k \frac{{q_1 q_2}}{{r^2}}
        $$
      </div>
      <div class="formula-buttons">
        <button class="formula-button">Explain Formula</button>
        <button class="formula-button">Open Formula Lab</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Explanation of Formula</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      <p>
      Coulomb's Law, as expressed in the formula above, quantifies the electrostatic force between two point charges.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>\(F\) represents the electrostatic force between the charges (measured in Newtons).</li>
        <li>\(k\) is Coulomb's constant, approximately equal to \(8.9875 \times 10^9 \, \text{N⋅m}^2/\text{C}^2\).</li>
        <li>\(q_1\) and \(q_2\) are the magnitudes of the charges (measured in Coulombs).</li>
        <li>\(r\) is the distance between the centers of the charges (measured in meters).</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
      The force is attractive if the charges have opposite signs (one positive and one negative), and repulsive if the charges have the same sign (both positive or both negative).  The force acts along the line connecting the two charges.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Applications</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      <ul>
        <li>Understanding the structure of atoms and molecules.</li>
        <li>Designing and analyzing electronic circuits.</li>
        <li>Predicting the behavior of charged particles in electric fields.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Advantages</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      <ul>
          <li>Provides a fundamental understanding of electrostatic interactions.</li>
          <li>Allows for quantitative calculations of forces between charges.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Disadvantages</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      <ul>
        <li>Only applies to point charges or spherically symmetric charge distributions.</li>
        <li>Does not account for relativistic effects.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Important Notes</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      <ul>
        <li>The force is inversely proportional to the square of the distance.</li>
        <li>The force is proportional to the product of the charges.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Summary</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      Coulomb's Law is a fundamental law in electrostatics that describes the force between charged particles. It is essential for understanding a wide range of phenomena in physics and engineering.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Suggested Questions</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      <ul>
        <li>What happens to the force if the distance between the charges is doubled?</li>
        <li>How does the sign of the charges affect the force?</li>
        <li>What is the role of Coulomb's constant (k)?</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
```

